or a bookshelf and \'Paid 265 of 270\', which is above the middle half of 260-270. Our buyers skip shops above the upper quartile.","expected":"A higher visit-to-deal conversion and more visits, since buyers filter by price. Margin per deal falls by roughly 2-5%, but volume rises, and the 1.4% share is far below the leaders\'.","risk":"If the reserve is close to the median the margin vanishes, and a lower price may attract more haggling that reduces margin further.","honest":"It uses only real prices that we actually charge and does not set a fake \'was\' price. It does not go below the reserve.","test":"Run the change for a few days. Compare share, deals per visit, and average paid-to-median ratio against the previous period, using the 15-minute meta_shop cycle for logging.","effort":"knob"},{"side":"shop","title":"Return-visit discount through a signed receipt number","what":"Put the receipt number in the thank-you message and give a small, real discount (about 3%, never below reserve+5) when a buyer cites a valid earlier receipt of ours on a later visit. Validate the number against our own signed receipts, one use per receipt.","evidence":"11im, the market leader, ends each deal with \'Receipt No. ...: mention it on your next visit for an extra 3% off.\' Its 1275 deals and 21.8% share show that repeat visits carry a lot of volume. Our loyalty code is one-time and tiered but sits in a separate mechanism.","expected":"More repeat visits, with a modest share gain. The cost is 3% on returning orders only, and the discount is applied only when it stays above the floor.","risk":"Overlap with the existing loyalty tiers and stamp card could stack discounts below reserve+5, so a cap is needed. It may also be abused by one buyer reusing a receipt.","honest":"The discount is real, honored every time, and not tied to reviews or stars. It is only a repeat-customer offer, and our own agents never trade with it.","test":"Count the share of orders that cite a receipt, the repeat rate per buyer id, and the average margin on those orders, compared with orders without a receipt over one week.","effort":"small code"},{"side":"buyer","title":"Raise the budget cap on walked deals and anchor at the median","what":"When a shop\'s ask is at or below the market median (and within the 1.1 x upper quartile cap), accept or close at the ask instead of making three counteroffers and walking. For the leading shops, make the first offer at the median minus a small step, and then jump to the median on the second offer, so each walk-away happens only if the ask is clearly above the median.","evidence":"Our buyer made 10 trades without a deal out of the last 40, e.g. \'lee-sungsoo chair budget 160 (walked)\', \'11im bookshelf budget 230 (walked)\' twice, \'psyoung82 bookshelf budget 230 (walked)\'. Those budgets (160 for a chair at 180, 230 for a bookshelf at 260) are below every price, so the walk is a budget problem, not a shop problem. Meanwhile 11im/lee-sungsoo/psyoung82 close at the median in 1-2 offers.","expected":"Fewer wasted trades. For budgets below the median, the buyer should skip the leading shops at once (no three-offer walk) and go to cheaper shops such as willow-goods (chair counter 198-216) or ggari-shop, which raises the deal rate from 30/40 toward about 35/40.","risk":"Skipping the top shops for budget reasons might lead to lower-rated shops or to shops with unsigned receipts, so the Round 2 signed-receipt filter must stay.","honest":"The buyer never pays above its budget and does not misstate its budget. It just selects shops by a true fact, the going price, before it starts negotiating.","test":"Track the deal rate and the average paid-to-budget ratio (86.4% now) over the next 40 trades, and count walk-aways by reason (budget below median vs shop refused).","effort":"small code"}]}'
