e repeat-visit rate and how many returning buyers use a receipt ID, before and after, and compare margin per deal.", "effort": "small code"}, {"side": "buyer", "title": "Walk-away fallback to a second shop and a lower-budget-friendly shortlist", "what": "When the budget is below every price at the preferred shop, do not walk after three offers. Move to the next shop on the shortlist that has a median near the going rate (11im, lee-sungsoo, psyoung82 or JangSeongWoong, all at lamp 100 / chair 180 / bookshelf 260). Open at about 90-95% of the going median for the item and use the 40% raise cap. Keep the 1.1x upper-quartile ceiling and the budget cap.", "evidence": "Our buyer\'s last 40 trades: 8 without a deal, all \'walked\' (11im lamp budget 90 twice, lee-sungsoo lamp budget 90, 11im bookshelf 230, JangSeongWoong bookshelf 230, 25620027 bookshelf 230). Going prices are lamp 100 and bookshelf 260, above these budgets. Paid is 84.5% of budget on average.", "expected": "Saves effort on trades that cannot close, and can raise the deal rate where a shop with a lower price exists. Does not raise paid price.", "risk": "If the budget is truly below all prices, a second shop wastes time and money for no deal. The extra visits add load to other shops.", "honest": "Only offers within budget and the 1.1x ceiling, and does not overload shops, since it makes at most one extra visit per task.", "test": "Compare the share of walked trades (8 of 40) before and after, and the extra visits per task.", "effort": "small code"}]}'
