efer shops with a high deal/visit ratio and walk less often", "what": "Add each shop\'s deals/visits ratio to the buyer\'s ranking, and stop retrying shops where we have repeatedly ended without a deal. Fair-furniture bookshelf (budget 230) walked three times and 11im ended \'None\' three times. Raise the walk threshold: if the shop\'s price is within the 1.1 x upper-quartile cap, make the final offer at the cap rather than walking.", "evidence": "8 of the last 40 trades ended without a deal, and fair-furniture bookshelf at budget 230 walked three times, which is far below the 260 median, so the budget could never have met it. nocha12 (74%), lee-sungsoo (77%) and 11im (72%) show high ratios, and the public ledger carries those numbers.", "expected": "A deal rate above 80% (32/40 now) with the same average price paid. Fewer wasted visits.", "risk": "A ratio can reflect easy buyers rather than shop quality. A small sample can mislead for small shops.", "honest": "The buyer uses public ledger data and its own memory and never offers above its budget.", "test": "Log the no-deal reasons for the next 40 trades. Compare the deal rate and the percent of budget paid against the current 80% and 83.4%.", "effort": "small code"}]}'
